#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""afield: keep Remote Control sessions across restarts and reach them from a terminal.

  afield                      open the tmux view: one window per session
  afield new [title]          start a new session in its own window (phone title optional)
  afield open <name|id>       same, focused on one session
  afield open --revive <id>   same, also for a forgotten or never-tracked session (by id)
  afield list                 every tracked session and its state
  afield list --all           also forgotten sessions and untracked recent chats (ids to --revive)
  afield status [--json]      services, Even Terminal, memory and how many sessions run
  afield config [key]         the settings in use (~/.config/afield/config.json + defaults)
  afield config set <key> <value...>
                              change a setting (checked; applied, or says what applying needs)
  afield config unset <key>   put a setting back to its default
  afield config help          every setting, its value and default, and what a change needs
  afield logs                 follow the supervisor's log (Ctrl-c to stop)
  afield reopen <name|id>     bring back a closed, failed or dormant session
  afield reopen --revive <id> same, also for a forgotten or never-tracked session (by id)
  afield forget <name|id>     stop tracking a session (its transcript stays)
  afield retire [name|id]     close its window and stop tracking it (with retire_archives on,
                                 also archive it in the app)
                                 (no argument: the session you run it from)
  afield adopt <id>           bring in a session running in another terminal; once it is
                                 idle it is stopped there and resumed in its own window
  afield adopt --revive <id>  same; if it is not running anywhere, revive it as above
  afield doctor               check every Claude Code / Even / tmux / systemd internal afield
                                 relies on (contract tests; CONTRACTS.md). Options: --e2e (also a
                                 throwaway session: creates and archives one phone chat),
                                 --candidate <claude binary> (check a version before switching to it),
                                 --update-snapshot (accept the current shapes as the new baseline)
  afield alert test           send a test message to alerts_url (config), where contract
                                 failures are sent
  afield --version            print afield's version
  afield supervise            the loop run by afield-supervisor.service

Every session in ~/afield runs as one interactive Claude in its own window of tmux server
`-L afield`, session `rc` (afield-tmux.service), started as

    even-terminal claude --resume <uuid> --remote-control

so the same process serves the terminal (the window), the phone (Remote Control) and the
Even G2 glasses (Even Terminal's wrapper registers it, and the glasses' prompts are typed into
it). Even Terminal is optional (install.sh --with-even); without it, or while it is down, sessions
start without the wrapper and go back on it once it is up and they are idle. In tmux:
Ctrl-b n / p next or previous session, Ctrl-b w to pick from a list, Ctrl-b d to leave.
Sessions keep running when you leave. Windows are named after the phone's title.

Phone-started sessions begin as headless children of the server. Once such a session is idle,
the supervisor moves it into a window: it stops the child, appends a `bridge-session` line naming
the session's existing phone link to the transcript (afield's only transcript write),
and starts it as above. The phone keeps the same entry and title (tested).

The server runs with --no-create-session-in-dir: a server-owned session gets reattached by
the server on restart, and that reattach rewrote a converted session's transcript (tested).
Sessions the earlier version left as `claude --bg` jobs are moved into windows the same way.
"""
import fcntl
import glob
import hashlib
import hmac
import json
import os
import re
import shlex
import signal
import socket
import subprocess
import sys
import threading
import time
import uuid as uuid_mod
import urllib.request
from datetime import datetime

VERSION = "0.1.0"
HOME = os.path.expanduser("~")

